# 1 bassline roles
mk("Z6IUhXyOvFc","I Fix A Beginner Producer's Weak Bass (in under 15 minutes)",
 "three-bassline-roles-clear-sub-40-80hz-lowcut-others-100-150hz-matching-rhythm-vs-eighth-pluck-pulse-vs-sustained-root-per-bar",
 "Clear-bass workflow: low-cut every other part at 100-150 Hz, keep bass notes at 40-80 Hz, then audition three bass-line roles (matching-rhythm, eighth-note pulsing pluck with saw plus same-octave sine and a duck, sustained root per bar) and pick the one that fits",
 "Give a track a clear, satisfying low end by first making room for the bass and then writing the bass line three ways from the chord roots: a matching bass that copies the melody's rhythm, a pulsing eighth-note pluck bass, and a sustained one-note-per-bar bass. Audition all three against the track and keep the one that fits best. The delta against our bass sound-design skills is the bass-line writing roles and the cleanup order, not the patch.",
 ["Any DAW with a piano roll and a spectrum analyzer","Any synth with a saw/sine oscillator, low-pass filter and a spare envelope","A sidechain/volume-duck tool or LFO on gain"],
 [("track","project","Song or loop with chords and a melody, before the bass is written",True),("chord_roots","list","Root note of each chord",True)],
 [("bass_options","midi+audio","Three bass tracks (matching, pulsing, sustained) for A/B choice"),("clean_mix","project","Other parts low-cut so the sub range belongs to the bass")],
 ["Do not layer several basses while learning; one clear bass is easier to mix.",
  "Keep other instruments out of the sub range: low-cut them somewhere around 100-150 Hz.",
  "Glide on the sustained bass is optional; if used, keep it short and only between notes, never as a random or LFO pitch wobble.",
  TASTE_P, TASTE_D, PS, A],
 [("Clean up first: mute or delete the old bass layers, thin out muddy duplicate chord layers, reduce heavy reverb on the lead/piano, and leave small gaps between melody notes so it reads more rhythmic.","DAW mixer / piano roll","A clear melody on its own, with space for a bass"),
  ("Low-cut every non-bass instrument at a sweet spot between about 100 and 150 Hz. Check the analyzer: nothing else should spill into the sub range.","EQ / analyzer","Sub range is empty except for the bass"),
  ("Make the bass patch: init synth, shift the octave until the fundamental sits below 100 Hz with notes landing between about 40 and 80 Hz, then use a tight low-pass to focus any waveform toward its lowest frequencies. Try several waveforms; add mid presence by opening the cutoff slightly or with light distortion.","synth","A full, focused bass tone in the 40-80 Hz zone"),
  ("Option 1, matching bass: copy the chord roots to a new track and cut them to the exact rhythm of the melody, so bass and melody hit together.","piano roll","Bass mirrors the melody rhythm and harmonizes it"),
  ("If any bass note gets lost in the chords or piano, move that note down an octave so the two parts sit in separate ranges.","piano roll","Every bass note stays audible and clear"),
  ("Option 2, pulsing bass: eighth notes (8 per bar) following the chord roots. Make it a pluck: put a spare envelope on a fully closed cutoff with sustain at zero; use a saw and add a sine at the same octave to thicken it; add a light sidechain-style duck to enhance the pulse.","synth + duck","Tight, driving pluck pulse on the roots (a 16th-note version moves twice as fast)"),
  ("Option 3, sustained bass: one bass note per bar on the chord root. If it holds the energy back, try mono with a short glide between notes, or a wider high-unison saw version, optionally with a filter envelope; remember a mono sub sounds more anchored.","synth","Relaxed, reliable foundation that works with almost any chords"),
  ("Play the track with each option and pick the one that fits: matching moves the melody along, pulsing gives a tighter foundation, sustained is the calmest.","DAW","One chosen bass role, the others kept as alternates")],
 ["No other track has significant energy below about 100 Hz on the analyzer.",
  "The bass fundamentals sit roughly between 40 and 80 Hz.",
  "Three bass versions exist (matching, pulsing eighths, sustained per bar) and all follow the chord roots.",
  "No bass note is masked by the chords; masked notes were moved down an octave."],
 ["bass","bassline","sub-bass","mix-clarity","arrangement","punchy-bass","steady-pitch"])

# 2 voice leading strata
mk("3cSsQw4CggE","The only trick you need for Epic Emotional Cinematic composing",
 "compress-expand-voice-leading-seconds-to-thirds-strata-a-perfect-consonance-apart-held-common-tone-preparation-modal-layers",
 "Cinematic voice-leading rule for pad/string layers: each two-voice layer alternates compressed seconds and expanded thirds, keeps one common tone between moves, and each new layer starts a perfect fourth, fifth or octave from the layer above, all inside one mode",
 "Write lush, emotional cinematic chord textures without analyzing harmony: build two-voice layers (strata) that alternate between a second and a third, keep one note held into each new interval, and stack further layers starting a perfect consonance (4th, 5th or octave) away from the layer above, staying inside one mode. Works for synth strings, pad voices or choir layers.",
 ["Any DAW or notation with a piano roll","Basic interval names: second, third, fourth, fifth, octave","A chosen mode/scale (the example is a modal F)"],
 [("mode","string","One scale/mode to stay inside (white-key mode works)",True),("layers","int","Number of strata (2-4 typical: e.g. high strings, low strings, woodwind-like line)",True)],
 [("voiced_texture","midi","Layered two-voice strata with prepared dissonances and contour"),("render","audio","Pads/strings playing the texture")],
 ["Stay inside a single mode; you do not need to know the chord names.",
  "Seconds are the compressed/dissonant state, thirds the imperfect-consonant release; alternate them.",
  "Synth voices playing this keep steady pitch: no random pitch wobble; vibrato, if any, slow and tiny.",
  "Composition rule only; renders with any pad, string or choir patch.",
  TASTE_D, A],
 [("Pick a mode and write the top layer as two voices that alternate a second (compressed) and a third (expanded), e.g. a second that falls out to a third.","piano roll","A two-voice line that breathes in and out"),
  ("On each move, keep one of the two notes from the previous interval (a held common tone or preparation) and move only the other.","piano roll","Each new dissonance sounds prepared and smooth"),
  ("Start the second layer a perfect consonance (4th, 5th or octave) below the top layer's top note, and run it in the opposite phase: where the top layer is compressed, this one starts expanded, and vice versa. Being less strict about common tones is OK here.","piano roll","Two strata that interact, giving thick, counterpoint-like chords"),
  ("Add further layers the same way (e.g. a scale-wise woodwind-like line), each starting a perfect consonance away from the layer above. Let some voices move in parallel and some in contrary motion.","piano roll","Rich, modal texture with contrary motion"),
  ("For a growing, expansive feeling, let the layers move wider and wider apart over the phrase.","piano roll","The texture opens up toward the climax"),
  ("Assign layers to separate pad/string voices and listen; adjust by ear rather than analysis.","synth/sampler","An emotional cinematic texture with prepared dissonances")],
 ["Every two-voice layer alternates seconds and thirds.",
  "Consecutive intervals in the top layer share one held note.",
  "Each layer starts a 4th, 5th or octave from the layer above.",
  "All notes belong to the chosen mode."],
 ["composition","voice-leading","harmony","cinematic","strings","pads","arrangement","modal"])

# 3 declutter arrangement + self-osc filter kick sub
mk("CwRN2ZVZweA","Ambient track walkthrough - how to make music like Martin Stürtzer",
 "mix-by-arrangement-ambient-techno-self-oscillating-filter-kick-sub-keytracked-tuned-to-root-pads-low-mid-high-rotate-out-16-vs-14-step-glitch-hp-kick-return",
 "Mix by arrangement in an ambient-techno track: a kick-plus-sub made only from a self-oscillating filter (snappy envelopes, sustain above zero, key-tracked, tuned to the root), a low and a mid/high pad pair, sequences on the same chord tones, and sections where pads rotate out so the kick, bass and main pad have room",
 "Build a clean ambient-techno arrangement where most of the mixing happens by choosing which parts play when. Make the kick and sub from a self-oscillating filter, voice pads as a low/high pair without a third, derive sequences from the same chord tones, and rotate pads out before kick, bass and the main pad enter. Use a high-passed kick before the drums return and end with pads muted and only the sequences left.",
 ["Synth with a self-oscillating resonant filter, filter key-tracking and separate filter/amp envelopes","DAW with clips/piano roll, delay and reverb","Bitcrusher and a beat-repeat/glitch effect with adjustable loop length (optional)"],
 [("key","string","Track root (example: F sharp)",True),("chord","list","Chord tones without a third, e.g. root, fifth, seventh, plus a fourth for sequences",True)],
 [("arrangement","project","Sectioned track with pads, sequences, kick+sub and percussion that never all play at once"),("kick_sub_patch","synth-preset","Self-oscillating filter kick with sub tail")],
 ["Tune the self-oscillating filter cutoff exactly to the root note, or the kick/sub will sound dissonant.",
  "Do not run every pad at once; the arrangement is the mix. The master chain stays nearly empty.",
  "Per-note random modulation may target FM amount or timbre only, never pitch. " + TASTE_P,
  TASTE_D + " The source brings the kick in after a pad intro; shorten that intro to about 8 counts.",
  "Rebuild pads from generic saw/sine unison patches; no third-party presets.", PS, A],
 [("Kick + sub: set all oscillator levels to zero so only the self-oscillating filter sounds. Give the filter envelope a very snappy decay, give the amp envelope a snappy decay with sustain above zero so a sub tone rings after the attack, and modulate the filter envelope amount by key tracking so higher notes hit higher.","synth","A punchy kick with a sustained tuned sub after the click"),
  ("Tune the filter cutoff to the track root. Write the kick clip with notes in up to three octaves (root plus an extra note two octaves up) for variation.","synth + clip","Kick and sub in key with the track"),
  ("Percussion: a short drum hit through a bitcrusher and a repeat/reverse glitch effect whose loop is 14 steps against the 16-step clip, then a little delay and reverb. Add a simple hi-hat on bar 3 later.","drum rack + FX","Shifting, self-varying percussion with no extra programming"),
  ("Pads as a pair: a mid/high chord pad of root, fifth and seventh with no third (major/minor stays ambiguous), held 4 bars then 4 bars of rest, through a slow delay and a big reverb; plus a very low pad on the root. The pair together sets the mood.","synth + delay/reverb","Spacious, ambiguous chord mood split across low and mid/high ranges"),
  ("Sequence: a single sine-like FM oscillator playing the same chord tones (root, fifth, seventh, plus the fourth) as a 4-bar improvised line; random per-note FM amount from two random LFOs, a little unison, a plucky envelope with short decay running into a longer release, filter fully open, delay and reverb on the insert. Optionally add a clock-synced repeat sparkle an octave up.","FM synth","Lush, gently varying pluck line built from the pad's notes"),
  ("Second sequence later: a lightly detuned saw unison patch with snappy filter and amp envelopes, written as a 3-bar pattern (not 4) so it shifts against the 4-bar sections; ride its filter cutoff by hand through the track.","synth","Evolving polymetric counter-line"),
  ("Arrange by decluttering: open with the pads, add the first sequence, then let one pad die out so only the sequence remains, which makes room for the kick, bass and main pad to enter. Keep the opening pads silent while the main pad plays.","arrangement","Each new part is clearly heard; nothing gets muddy"),
  ("Rotate: after the main pad plays twice, bring the opening pads back with kick and bass; then add the percussion and a simple high pad of three long notes to open the top range, muting the other pads.","arrangement","The arrangement opens up without piling layers"),
  ("Break and return: stop kick and bass for a while; before they return, high-pass the kick for a bar or two, then bring them back full. Use the same high-pass before the final fade.","filter automation","A tension-and-release break with a smooth drum re-entry"),
  ("Ending: mute all pads and let only the two sequences play out.","arrangement","A light, sequence-only outro")],
 ["The kick/sub patch has all oscillators at zero, and its tone is in tune with the track root.",
  "At no point are all pads playing together; the opening pads are silent while the main pad plays.",
  "The glitch loop length differs from the clip length (14 vs 16 steps), and the second sequence is 3 bars long.",
  "The kick is high-passed for the bars before each return.",
  "With Matt's taste applied, the kick enters by about 12 s."],
 ["arrangement","song-structure","sections","drums","kick","sub-bass","self-oscillating-filter","pads","ambient-techno","polymeter","drums-early"])

# 4 flanger sequence pad
mk("QdveXtMhAnk","Tutorial #36 | Flanger Sequence Pads",
 "flanger-sequence-pad-tri-plus-pulse-octave-1-bar-fade-lfo-static-flanger-time-stepped-by-square-lfo-vocoder-formant-slow-env-resample-down-swelling-fx-transition",
 "Flanger sequence pad: triangle plus a pulse one octave up whose level fades out and in once per bar, low-pass, a flanger with its own rate at zero whose delay time is stepped by a square/synced LFO for an arpeggio-like comb movement, then resample pitched down with rising delay/reverb mix for a transition",
 "Make a pad on held chords that moves like an arpeggio without playing extra notes: freeze the flanger's own sweep and step its delay time with a square or saw LFO so the comb peaks jump between positions. Then resample it slowed down into an ambient transition whose delay/reverb mix rises as the sample plays.",
 ["Any synth with two oscillators, a low-pass filter, LFOs that can be retriggered or tempo-synced, and a flanger whose rate can be zero (or a modulated short delay with feedback)","Optional vocoder/formant effect and a sampler or audio track for resampling"],
 [("chords","midi","A looping chord pattern; this is a chord pad, single notes sound thin",True),("tempo","bpm","Project tempo for the synced LFOs",True)],
 [("sequence_pad","synth-preset","Pad with stepped flanger movement"),("transition_fx","audio","Pitched-down resample with rising wet tail")],
 ["Keep the flanger's own rate at zero; all movement comes from the stepped delay-time LFO.",
  "Pitch stays steady (Matt's taste): add a few ms of slew on the stepped flanger-time LFO so steps do not click or pitch-glitch, and keep unison detune low and static on the main oscillator. No random pitch modulation.",
  "Resampling down an octave is a deliberate, fixed transposition for transitions, not a wobble.",
  TASTE_D, PS, A],
 [("Osc A: triangle with about 3 unison voices and low detune (it plays all the time). Osc B: pulse/square one octave up, level at zero, slightly more voices/detune.","synth","Mellow base with a brighter octave layer ready"),
  ("Put an LFO on osc B's level at a rate of about one bar, retriggered, with a shape that fades out and back in (or drops fast and comes back up slowly).","LFO","The octave layer breathes once per bar"),
  ("Low-pass the sound for a mellow tone, with key tracking; filter the harsher upper oscillator more if needed.","filter","Soft pad, upper layer not harsh"),
  ("Add a flanger at full mix (or high), set its own rate to zero, and modulate its delay time (depth/time) with a second LFO: a square wave as an on/off switch, or a tempo-synced 1/8 saw, unipolar, at a small amount. Find a sweet spot by ear, then adjust flanger feedback and phase.","flanger + LFO","Comb peaks jump between positions, giving arpeggio-like movement on a held chord"),
  ("Optionally add a vocoder/formant effect and adjust bandwidth, formant and band count for color. Lengthen the amp attack and release to remove sharp clicks.","FX + amp env","Smoother, more ambient pad with no clicking"),
  ("Record the pad. For a transition, resample it pitched down (slower), place it before a section change, send it to delay and reverb, automate both FX mixes to rise as the sample plays, and fade the sample's level from 0 up to about half.","sampler + automation","A swelling transition that ends in pure delay/reverb tail")],
 ["The flanger's free-running rate is zero; movement stops if the time LFO is disabled.",
  "Osc B's level visibly cycles once per bar.",
  "No audible clicks at the flanger steps.",
  "In the transition, the delay/reverb mix ends higher than it starts, and the tail continues after the sample ends."],
 ["pads","flanger","phaser-pad","modulation","comb","transitions","resampling","fx"])

# 5 allpass phaser
mk("1s_wcjB31iA","How to build a basic Phaser Effect | Max for Live Tutorial",
 "build-stereo-phaser-from-allpass-chain-1-7-stages-dry-plus-wet-half-gain-q-0-7071-center-500hz-exponential-lfo-16x-depth-right-channel-phase-offset-180",
 "Build a phaser from scratch: a chain of 1-7 all-pass biquads summed with the dry signal at half gain to make notches, Q 0.7071 for 'spread', a center frequency (50 Hz-18 kHz, start 500 Hz) swept exponentially by a 0.05-30 Hz sine/triangle LFO, a per-channel LFO phase offset up to 180 degrees and a dry/wet mix",
 "Implement your own stereo phaser in any patching environment or Web Audio (BiquadFilterNode type 'allpass'): phase-shift the signal through a chain of all-pass filters, add it back to the dry signal to create moving notches, and sweep the shared center frequency exponentially with an LFO offset between left and right. This gives Polyrhythm Studio a native phaser for slow phaser pads.",
 ["A patching or audio-graph environment with all-pass biquads (Web Audio BiquadFilterNode 'allpass', Max, Pd, any modular)","An LFO source (phasor ramp mapped to sine/triangle)"],
 [("input","audio","Pad or any signal to phase",True),("stages","int","Number of all-pass stages, 1-7 (more stages means more notches)",True)],
 [("phaser","audio-effect","Stereo phaser with order, spread, center, rate, depth, offset and dry/wet controls"),("phased_pad","audio","Pad with slow moving notches")],
 ["Sweep the center frequency exponentially (multiply/divide around the center), not linearly.",
  "A phaser only moves notches (all-pass phase), so pitch stays steady. For pads use slow rates (well under 1 Hz) to suit Matt's steady, calm taste.",
  "Selecting the stage count is 1-based (1-7); do not add an extra +1 offset.",
  TASTE_D, PS, A],
 [("Chain up to seven all-pass biquad filters (two-pole all-pass coefficients) and use a selector so only the chosen number of stages (1-7, the 'order' or notch count) is in the path.","all-pass chain","A phase-shifted copy of the input"),
  ("Add the phase-shifted signal to the original and multiply the sum by 0.5 for gain matching.","sum + gain","Notches appear in the spectrum where the copies cancel"),
  ("Controls for the filters: 'spread' = Q (range up to about 16, start at 0.7071 = square root of 0.5; higher means narrower notches) and 'center' frequency (50 Hz to about 18 kHz, start 500 Hz).","parameters","Notch width and position can be set"),
  ("Duplicate the whole chain for the right channel so left and right are processed separately.","stereo","Independent L/R phasers"),
  ("Build the LFO from a 0-1 ramp (phasor) at 0.05-30 Hz, starting at 1 Hz: map it to a triangle with a centered peak or to a sine, selectable.","LFO","Smooth periodic control signal"),
  ("Sweep exponentially: center × 16^(LFO × depth), so at the LFO maximum the center is multiplied by a factor and at the minimum divided by the same factor. Scale with a depth control.","modulation math","Notches sweep evenly across octaves"),
  ("For stereo, add an offset to the right channel's ramp before the wave shaper and wrap it with modulo 1. Scale the offset control by 0.5 so 100% equals 180 degrees.","stereo offset","Left and right notches move in opposite phase at full offset"),
  ("Add dry/wet: multiply the processed path by wet and the dry path by (1 - wet) per channel, then sum.","mix","Blendable phaser; at 0% wet the signal is untouched")],
 ["With depth at 0, the notches are static at the center frequency.",
  "Raising the stage count adds visible notches on a spectrum analyzer.",
  "At 100% offset the L and R notch sweeps are 180 degrees apart.",
  "The sweep is exponential: equal LFO steps move the notches by equal musical intervals."],
 ["phaser","phaser-pad","dsp","web-audio","allpass","modulation","stereo-width","fx","polyrhythm-studio"])
